summary.text:
The task is to define the primary symbolic meaning of "Night" in Christian teaching and biblical interpretation, based on independent knowledge.
{
  "primary_meaning": "Night symbolizes the realm of darkness, sin, evil, ignorance, and the absence of God's light and presence.

content.text:
{
  "primary_meaning": "Spiritual darkness, evil, sin, ignorance, or the absence of God's light and presence (in contrast to day/light)",
  "primary_basis": [
    "Romans 13:12",
    "1 Thessalonians 5:5-7",
    "John 3:19-20",
    "John 9:4",
    "John 11:10",
    "Ephesians 5:8-11",
    "Revelation 21:25; 22:5"
  ],
  "alternatives": [
    {
      "meaning": "A time of divine guidance, protection, or God's presence (e.g., pillar of fire)",
      "basis": [
        "Exodus 13:21-22",
        "Psalm 121:6",
        "Psalm 16:7"
      ],
      "prominence": "common"
    },
    {
      "meaning": "A season of trial, suffering, weeping, or waiting for deliverance",
      "basis": [
        "Psalm 30:5",
        "Isaiah 21:11-12",
        "Job 7:3-4",
        "Psalm 77:2-6"
      ],
      "prominence": "common"
    },
    {
      "meaning": "The present evil age or time before the full dawning of God's kingdom / second coming",
      "basis": [
        "Romans 13:11-12",
        "1 Thessalonians 5:1-8"
      ],
      "prominence": "minority"
    },
    {
      "meaning": "Rest, sleep, or natural cessation of work (sometimes extended to death)",
      "basis": [
        "Psalm 4:8",
        "John 9:4",
        "Psalm 127:2"
      ],
      "prominence": "minority"
    }
  ],
  "has_settled_consensus": true,
  "confidence": "high",
  "notes": "In Christian biblical interpretation, night is most consistently and prominently used as a foil to light/day (God, Christ, truth, righteousness). Positive or neutral uses exist but are secondary to the darkness/evil motif in didactic and apocalyptic teaching. No single exhaustive meaning covers every occurrence; context determines valence."
}
